1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,150
Here's how to reverse engineer Harvest from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:08,100
Claude passed all twenty-one checks, but my spec never said who could reassign a waiting timesheet.

3
00:00:08,917 --> 00:00:10,837
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:11,007 --> 00:00:14,783
It copies the part of Harvest a small team actually needs to bill by the hour.

5
00:00:15,018 --> 00:00:18,069
Clients and projects, with an hourly rate and a budget.

6
00:00:18,218 --> 00:00:21,240
A weekly timesheet for each person, in fifteen-minute steps.

7
00:00:21,446 --> 00:00:24,092
Submission to their manager, whose approval locks the week.

8
00:00:24,476 --> 00:00:26,996
And an export of approved hours for invoicing.

9
00:00:28,083 --> 00:00:29,789
The core rule is the lock.

10
00:00:30,045 --> 00:00:33,373
A week moves from draft, to submitted, to approved.

11
00:00:33,629 --> 00:00:39,980
Approving copies each entry's rate onto the entry, so a later rate change never changes hours you may already have invoiced.

12
00:00:40,186 --> 00:00:45,988
Raise the Website redesign rate to one hundred and sixty dollars, and Lee's approved weeks still bill at one hundred and fifty.

13
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:49,524
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

14
00:00:49,738 --> 00:00:52,490
The foundation, with people, projects and sign-in.

15
00:00:52,724 --> 00:00:54,666
My week, with submit and withdraw.

16
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,820
Approvals, rates and budgets.

17
00:00:57,076 --> 00:00:59,333
Then reminders, reports and the export.

18
00:00:59,498 --> 00:01:04,142
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

19
00:01:05,217 --> 00:01:06,028
Here's what it built.

20
00:01:06,284 --> 00:01:09,804
Lee's week shows forty hours, approved by Maya Chen and locked.

21
00:01:10,038 --> 00:01:13,812
Maya's approvals list has Jo Park's week, thirty-eight hours, waiting for her.

22
00:01:13,958 --> 00:01:19,526
Projects shows Website redesign at eighty percent of its budget, ninety-six of one hundred and twenty hours.

23
00:01:19,846 --> 00:01:22,811
Reports split approved and submitted hours by client.

24
00:01:23,046 --> 00:01:25,564
And on a phone, you enter one day at a time.

25
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:32,971
In one final run, on a fresh database, all twenty-one checks passed, along with one hundred and twenty-three automated tests.

26
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:36,013
But the more useful result is what broke.

27
00:01:36,312 --> 00:01:38,808
The build exposed twenty-one gaps in the spec.

28
00:01:38,957 --> 00:01:40,429
Places where Claude had to guess.

29
00:01:40,664 --> 00:01:44,373
Sign-in was limited to the company's email domain, but no setting held the domain.

30
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:49,298
The hours field read like a picker, yet a check typed one forty and expected a refusal.

31
00:01:49,490 --> 00:01:53,308
And a note starting with an equals sign would run as a formula in the export.

32
00:01:53,671 --> 00:01:55,582
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

33
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:59,061
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

34
00:01:59,253 --> 00:02:04,607
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the hourly job on a real scheduler, or hosting.

35
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:07,012
Those are yours to check before your team relies on it.

36
00:02:08,117 --> 00:02:09,610
So is it worth building?

37
00:02:09,909 --> 00:02:14,026
Harvest's Teams plan starts at nine dollars per seat per month, billed annually.

38
00:02:14,303 --> 00:02:18,015
But timesheet approvals are listed on Enterprise, from fourteen dollars per seat.

39
00:02:18,142 --> 00:02:21,790
For ten people, that's one thousand six hundred and eighty dollars a year.

40
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:26,206
If you want timers, mobile apps and invoicing in one tool, buy Harvest.

41
00:02:26,462 --> 00:02:29,482
If you only need hours approved and ready to invoice, build it.

42
00:02:30,583 --> 00:02:31,906
Everything is in one file.

43
00:02:32,162 --> 00:02:39,211
The data model, fourteen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty-one checks, with the twenty-one fixes already in.

44
00:02:40,167 --> 00:02:43,608
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

45
00:02:43,608 --> 00:02:45,708
If you build it, tell me what broke.

46
00:02:45,708 --> 00:02:46,175
I'm Sam.

47
00:02:46,175 --> 00:02:47,167
See you tomorrow.

